    "All tests pass with no long lines. Let me do a final review of the complete change.",
    "Let me confirm the exact reported reproduction end-to-end via the CLI runner.",
    "The fix is complete and verified.\n\n**Root cause:** In `Option.__init__`, the logic that derives the flag's stored value (`flag_value = not self.default`) was nested inside `if is_flag and type is None:`. So when an explicit `type=` was supplied, `flag_value` stayed `None`, and `add_to_parser` registered `const=None` for the single-option flag case \u2014 meaning `--foo` stored `None` instead of toggling the default.\n\n**Fix (`src/click/core.py`):** Moved the `flag_value = not self.default` derivation out so it applies whenever `is_flag` is set and no `flag_value` was given, regardless of whether an explicit `type` was supplied. Type re-guessing still only happens when `type is None`.\n\n**Tests (`tests/test_options.py`):** Added `test_flag_with_explicit_type_toggles_default` (parametrized over `type=bool`, `type=bool, default=True`, `type=click.BOOL`) and `test_flag_with_explicit_non_bool_type_uses_flag_value` (`type=str`). Both fail before the fix and pass after.\n\n**Verification:** All reported cases now produce the expected values (`--foo` \u2192 `True`; `default=True` \u2192 `False`; `type=click.BOOL` \u2192 `True`; `type=str` \u2192 `True`), omitted-flag behavior is unchanged, and the full suite passes: 723 passed, 22 skipped, 1 xfailed.\n\nDONE"
   ],
